.TH DANBY-HYDROGEN 1
.SH NAME
danby-hydrogen \- the radial wave function of a hydrogen-like atom
.SH SYNOPSIS
.B danby/hydrogen
.SH MODEL
.IR Danby-schrodinger (1)
shoots a particle in a made-up well.  This shoots the real one: an
electron in the Coulomb field of a nucleus, the only atom with an exact
answer and the reason anyone believed the theory.
.PP
In atomic units, with u(r) = r R(r),
.PP
.EX
u'' = [ l(l+1)/r^2 - 2Z/r - 2E ] u
.EE
.PP
and the bound states sit at
.PP
.EX
E = -Z^2 / 2n^2,   n = l+1, l+2, ...
.EE
.PP
and nowhere else.  For Z = 1 that is \-0.5, then \-0.125, then \-0.0556:
the Lyman and Balmer numbers, which is to say the colours of hydrogen.
.SH DESCRIPTION
.B Danby/hydrogen
is a
.IR danbylab (2)
script and nothing else \(em the whole model is the text of
.BR /appl/danby/hydrogen.spec .
.SS What to look for
The energy slider steps by 0.0025, so \-0.5 and \-0.125 are both exactly
reachable.  Land on one and the tail comes down; a single notch either
side and it runs away, by a factor of 1.6 million at the ground state
and by 176 at 2s.
.PP
.B Charge
is the Z of a hydrogen-LIKE ion, and the levels go as Z squared: halve
it and the ground state moves from \-0.5 to \-0.125.  That is a check
the test makes, and it is also singly-ionised helium seen backwards.
.PP
.B Angular_momentum
is what this model has that the well does not.
.I l(l+1)/r^2
is the centrifugal barrier, and going inwards it climbs faster than the
Coulomb attraction falls, so an electron with angular momentum is held
off the nucleus: a wall grows on the left of the diagram and the density
stops reaching r = 0.  It is why the ground state must have l = 0, and
why the lowest level for a given l is n = l+1 rather than n = 1 \(em at
l = 1 there is no state at \-0.5 at all, only at \-0.125 and above.
.PP
A non-integer l is allowed and is not a state of anything.  The slider
steps by 0.02 so every integer is reachable.
.SS The diagram
The effective potential \(em Coulomb plus centrifugal \(em with the
energy drawn across it, and a marker showing how far the march has got.
Nothing in it is a particle and nothing moves; see
.IR danby-schrodinger (1),
which says the same thing at more length.
.SH SOURCE
.B /appl/danby/hydrogen.spec
.SH SEE ALSO
.IR danby (1),
.IR danby-schrodinger (1),
.IR danby-rabi (1),
.IR danbylab (2),
.IR danbylabtest (1)
.SH BUGS
Shooting outward into a classically forbidden region is unstable, and
hydrogen is far worse for it than a harmonic well: the ground state
decays like e^-r across the whole range, so the growing solution is
amplified against it by e^40.  Three numbers here were measured rather
than chosen, and all three are load-bearing.
.PP
The march starts from the SERIES, u = r^(l+1)(1 - Zr/(l+1)), and not
from the leading term alone.  That one extra term leaves an error of
order r0 squared rather than r0, and since that error is what seeds the
growing solution it is worth a factor of a hundred: without it the
ground-state tail at the wall is 12.8, against a peak of 0.37 that it is
meant to sit under.
.PP
.B Start_radius
is 0.001 and not smaller.  Contamination keeps falling as r0 squared all
the way down, but the equation stiffens as 1/r, and below about 0.001
the library's own 0.005 step can no longer resolve the inner region and
the error comes back: at 0.0001 the tail is 0.44, worse than at 0.001
and larger than the peak.  That optimum does not exist at a finer step,
which is why it had to be measured at the step the model actually runs
at and not at a comfortable one.
.PP
The wall is at r = 20 and the choice is a compromise.  Nearer, the
excited states cannot be told apart \(em one notch off 2s changes the
tail by 8 at r = 16 against 176 at r = 20.  Further, the ground state's
picture is ruined: its tail is 2.6 per cent of its own peak at r = 20
and 17 per cent at r = 22.  There is no wall that is best for both, and
a state much above n = 2 is simply outside this one.
.PP
n = 3, at \-0.0556, is not a multiple of the slider's 0.0025 and cannot
be landed on exactly, only approached.
.PP
.IR Danbylabtest (1)
checks the three lowest levels with a control a notch either side, the
Z-squared scaling, the drawn potential against the formula, and that
raising l lifts it.  It cannot check the inner samples of that potential
against anything: at Z = 1 every sample inside r = 1.667 is below the
drawing floor and clamps, and a clamped point tests the clamp and
nothing about its own coefficients.
